<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author"><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>California Institute of Technology<sup id="id5" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>Equal contribution<span id="id6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{alexrbranch, sunaybhat1, jimmery}@ucla.edu, pottie@ee.ucla.edu</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">PureVQ-GAN</span>, a defense against data poisoning that forces backdoor triggers through a discrete bottleneck using Vector-Quantized VAE with GAN discriminator. By quantizing poisoned images through a learned codebook, <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PureVQ-GAN</span> destroys fine-grained trigger patterns while preserving semantic content. A GAN discriminator ensures outputs match the natural image distribution, preventing reconstruction of out-of-distribution perturbations. On CIFAR-10, <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PureVQ-GAN</span> achieves 0% poison success rate (PSR) against Gradient Matching and Bullseye Polytope attacks, and 1.64% against Narcissus—while maintaining 91-95% clean accuracy. Unlike diffusion-based defenses requiring hundreds of iterative refinement steps, <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PureVQ-GAN</span> is over 50× faster, making it practical for real training pipelines.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Data poisoning attacks compromise machine learning models by injecting malicious training samples that cause targeted misclassification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Backdoor attacks are a critical subclass where models behave normally on clean inputs but produce attacker-chosen outputs when a trigger is present. Clean-label attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> are especially insidious - the poisoned samples are correctly labeled and visually indistinguishable from clean data, evading human inspection and statistical filtering.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attack Mechanisms:</span> Data poisoning attacks inject <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-bounded adversarial perturbations <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\delta\|_{\infty}\leq\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\delta\|_{\infty}\leq\xi</annotation></semantics></math> (typically <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi=8/255" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>255</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi=8/255</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16/255" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>255</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16/255</annotation></semantics></math>) into a small fraction <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (typically 1%) of training data. Both attack categories operate within this constraint to maintain imperceptibility:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Triggered attacks</span> embed a specific trigger <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\rho\|_{\infty}\leq\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\rho\|_{\infty}\leq\xi</annotation></semantics></math> that causes misclassification to target label <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\text{adv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\text{adv}}</annotation></semantics></math> when present at inference. Narcissus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> exemplifies clean-label backdoor attacks where poisoned samples appear visually normal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Triggerless attacks</span> add perturbations <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\epsilon\|_{\infty}\leq\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\epsilon\|_{\infty}\leq\xi</annotation></semantics></math> to align poisoned samples’ feature representations with target images. Gradient Matching (GM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> optimizes poisons to match target gradients during training, while Bullseye Polytope (BP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> uses a fine-tuned generator to cluster poisons around targets in feature space. These attacks achieve targeted misclassification without requiring triggers at inference time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Current defenses have fundamental limitations. Outlier detection methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> fail against clean-label attacks where poisons blend with normal data distributions. Certified defenses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> provide theoretical guarantees but sacrifice significant accuracy (&gt;10% drop). Compression-based methods like JPEG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and quantization degrade image quality, harming downstream task performance. Recent generative purifiers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> show promise but rely on expensive iterative sampling - requiring hundreds of iterative refinement steps per image.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">PureVQ-GAN</span>, a novel defense that leverages the discrete bottleneck of Vector-Quantized VAEs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> combined with adversarial training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Our key insight: forcing representations through a finite codebook of learned prototypes destroys adversarial perturbations while preserving semantic content. Unlike continuous autoencoders that can propagate subtle poison signals, our discrete quantization creates a hard information bottleneck that attackers cannot bypass.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main Contributions:</span> (1) <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficient purification:</span> <span id="S1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PureVQ-GAN</span> is over 50× faster than iterative generative defenses. (2) <span id="S1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">State-of-the-art results:</span> 0% PSR against GM/BP, 1.64% against Narcissus, while maintaining 91-95% clean accuracy on CIFAR-10. (3) <span id="S1.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scalable architecture:</span> Demonstrated with models scaling to 300M parameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work - Generative Purifiers</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work directly builds off of the PureGen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> framework. PureGen proposes training and then sampling from a generative model to purify train time perturbation based attacks. Previously, PureGen proposed initializing an MCMC chain of an EBM or diffusion model with a poisoned image and sampling around the distribution of the learned image space until the poisons are erased.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While EBMs and Diffusion models are strong purifiers, they are computationally expensive. EBMs require gradients at inference time, and Diffusion models demand hundreds of iterative denoising steps, making both methods impractical for large-scale training pipelines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, as an alternative to EBMs and Diffusion models, we utilize a VQ-GAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> for defense. We hypothesize this works well, as the discrete bottleneck of a VQ-VAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> filters out poison perturbations, while the GAN loss maintains sharp, realistic outputs. To our knowledge, this is the first work to successfully apply a VQ-GAN as an efficient, high-fidelity poison purifier.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method: <span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PureVQ-GAN</span></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Architecture</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PureVQ-GAN</span> combines a Vector-Quantized Variational Autoencoder (VQ-VAE) generator <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with an adversarial discriminator <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{adv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{adv}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The encoder <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> maps input <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math> to continuous latent representation <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{e}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times w\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{e}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times w\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each spatial location in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> is quantized to its nearest codebook vector:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{q}=\arg\min_{e_{k}\in\{e_{1},...,e_{K}\}}\|z_{e}(x)-e_{k}\|_{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{q}=\arg\min_{e_{k}\in\{e_{1},...,e_{K}\}}\|z_{e}(x)-e_{k}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where codebook <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> learnable <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vectors. The decoder <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> reconstructs <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}=D(e_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}=D(e_{q})</annotation></semantics></math> from quantized latents. This discrete bottleneck is critical: poison perturbations cannot precisely control which codebook vectors are selected, breaking the attack.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.25792v1/figures/vqpure_architecture.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/90;" width="333" height="90" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">PureVQ-GAN<span id="S3.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> architecture.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Training Objective</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PureVQ-GAN</span> with three loss components:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reconstruction Loss:</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{rec}}=\|x-\hat{x}\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{rec}}=\|x-\hat{x}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> ensures faithful reconstruction of clean images.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Codebook Learning:</span> Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, we use:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{VQ}}=\beta\|z_{e}-\text{sg}[e_{q}]\|_{2}^{2}+\|\text{sg}[z_{e}]-e_{q}\|_{2}^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>VQ</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>sg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>sg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{VQ}}=\beta\|z_{e}-\text{sg}[e_{q}]\|_{2}^{2}+\|\text{sg}[z_{e}]-e_{q}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sg}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>sg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sg}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> is stop-gradient. The first term (commitment loss with <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.25</annotation></semantics></math>) encourages encoder outputs to stay close to codebook vectors. The second term updates codebook vectors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adversarial Loss:</span> To maintain perceptual quality, we add a discriminator:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{GAN}}=\mathbb{E}_{x}[\log D_{\text{adv}}(x)]+\mathbb{E}_{x}[\log(1-D_{\text{adv}}(\hat{x}))]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>GAN</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{GAN}}=\mathbb{E}_{x}[\log D_{\text{adv}}(x)]+\mathbb{E}_{x}[\log(1-D_{\text{adv}}(\hat{x}))]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Total Generator Loss:</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{G}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{rec}}+\mathcal{L}_{\text{VQ}}+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{\text{GAN}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>VQ</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>GAN</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{G}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{rec}}+\mathcal{L}_{\text{VQ}}+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{\text{GAN}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> controls the balance between adversarial strength and accuracy. We set <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> in our experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details:</span> Codebook size <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=512</annotation></semantics></math> balances expressiveness and regularization. Full training hyperparameters and architecture details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Implementation Details ‣ PureVQ-GAN: Defending Data Poisoning Attacks through Vector-Quantized Bottlenecks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Defense Deployment</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Phase:</span> Train <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PureVQ-GAN</span> on the training dataset, which may contain poisoned samples (we use the same poisoned training set in our experiments). The GAN discriminator learns to distinguish real training images from reconstructions, effectively learning the natural image distribution from the overwhelming majority (99%) of clean samples. During classifier training, pass each mini-batch through <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PureVQ-GAN</span> before computing gradients.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference Phase:</span> Purify test images before classification using a single forward pass. For potentially stronger attacks, multiple refinement passes can be applied:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(t+1)}=G(x^{(t)}),\quad x^{(0)}=x_{\text{poisoned}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>poisoned</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(t+1)}=G(x^{(t)}),\quad x^{(0)}=x_{\text{poisoned}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">though our experiments show one pass is sufficient for state-of-the-art defense.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theoretical Insight:</span> The discrete bottleneck creates an information bottleneck <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> that acts as a filter - sufficient for natural images but insufficient for encoding both image content and poison signal. Critically, the GAN discriminator provides robustness to poisoned training data: since poisoned samples constitute only a small fraction (typically 1%) of the training set, the discriminator learns the natural image distribution from the overwhelming majority of clean samples. During reconstruction, the adversarial loss enforces that outputs match this learned clean distribution, effectively rejecting adversarial perturbations even if they were present in the training data. The discrete codebook quantization combined with the distributional constraint from the GAN creates a dual defense mechanism that purifies poisoned inputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PureVQ-GAN</span> on CIFAR-10 against three state-of-the-art clean-label attacks:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attack Setup:</span> (1) <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gradient Matching (GM)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> - a clean-label attack where poisons are optimized to match the gradient of a target image during training. (2) <span id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Narcissus (NS)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> - a backdoor attack introducing a specific patterned trigger designed to be stealthy. (3) <span id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bullseye Polytope (BP)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> - a powerful clean-label backdoor where a fine-tuned generator produces poisons that cluster around a target image in feature space. Each attack poisons 1% of training data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Protocol:</span> We train ResNet-18 classifiers on purified datasets. <span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PureVQ-GAN</span> uses encoder-decoder with codebook size K=512, trained on the same poisoned CIFAR-10 dataset (containing 1% poisoned samples) used for classifier training—matching the PureGen-EBM/Diffusion training protocol. All results use a single forward pass through the purifier. Main results (Table 1) use our 300M parameter model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main Results:</span> <span id="S4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PureVQ-GAN</span> achieves near-perfect defense. Against GM and BP, we achieve 0% PSR while maintaining &gt;91% clean accuracy. For the harder NS attack, PSR is only 1.64% with 94.61% clean accuracy - the highest among defenses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline Comparison:</span> EPIC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> shows limited success with PSR ranging from 10-42% and degrades accuracy to as low as 81.95%. JPEG achieves strong defense (0-1.78% PSR) with accuracy around 90-93%. PureGen-EBM/Diffusion achieve comparable PSR but require hundreds of iterative refinement steps. FRIENDS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> reduces PSR to 0% for GM and 8% for BP, with 8.32% against NS. See Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Additional Experimental Details ‣ PureVQ-GAN: Defending Data Poisoning Attacks through Vector-Quantized Bottlenecks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for detailed qualitative comparison.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T1.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.7pt 5.0pt;"></td>
